# @podlite/schema

=head1 Upcoming

=item hold a C«=config» to the block it is written in, so a declaration inside one block no longer configures the blocks that follow it

=item keep a cell built from CSV or TSV as text, so a value reading C«Z<secret>» or C«C<command>» reaches the output whole; C«:allow» on the block names the codes that act inside such a cell

=item read configuration attributes on C«L<>» and C«W<>», so C«L<API|https://api.example.com :new :title('Doc')>» carries them into the AST instead of dumping them into the link specification

=item keep the closing angle of a markup code that ends with an attribute value in angles, C«L<Guide|https://example.fr/guide :lang<fr>>»

=item render the link attributes: C<:new>, C<:title>, C<:lang> and C<:download> reach html and jsx, C<:title> reaches markdown

=head1 0.0.59

=item read a markup code delimited by a run of angles, C«C<<…>>» and C«C<<<…>>>», so a closing angle can sit inside the content

=item export a document holding the space-preserving code instead of stopping on it; the markdown and html exporters read its content as text now

=head1 0.0.58

=item a table row written on one line and separated by whitespace now splits on two spaces, as the specification says — the positional template it used to go through needed three, and a row with tighter columns collapsed into a single cell without a word
=item such a cell no longer carries the spaces that aligned it in the source
=item a heading keeps its own text as its identifier; shaping it for a url — collapsing separators, dropping punctuation, numbering repeats — moved to the output side, where each format applies its own rules
=item stop transliterating cyrillic headings: «Приветствие Мир» kept its letters instead of becoming Privetstvie-Mir, and the original name is recoverable again
=item headings that shape into the same fragment get numbered instead of sharing one anchor
=item markdown export shapes a same-document link the way a markdown reader will: lower case, punctuation dropped, each remaining space a hyphen, repeats numbered from C<-1>. Html keeps its own rule, where case survives
=item html export writes the shaped fragment as the heading anchor instead of the raw name — C<< <h2 id="Getting Started"> >> carried a space
=item html and markdown exports send a link to the heading it names: C<< L<text|#Getting Started> >> reaches C<#Getting-Started>, and a target written in another case still finds it
=item anchors are handed out in one walk before rendering, so a heading keeps the same anchor however often a renderer asks for it and the numbering no longer skips
=item add C<indexAnchors>, C<findAnchor> and C<resolveFragment>: a link target inside the document finds its heading by name, exact match first and then without regard to case

=head1 0.0.57

=item a link keeps its target when the display text carries formatting codes — C<< L<I<text>|target> >> previously left the separator and target inside the text

=head1 0.0.56

=item B<Breaking:> square brackets and bare text in parentheses are no longer attribute values. C<:tags[a,b]> and C<:mime-type(text/csv)> now drop the attribute and report it; write C<< :tags<a b> >> and C<:mime-type('text/csv')>

=item report a directive line the parser could not read; each diagnostic now carries a code, C<value-unreadable> or C<directive-unreadable>

=item a value in brackets may continue on the next configuration line, the one starting with C<=> and a space; a quoted or angle-bracket value stays on its line, and a value left open is dropped with a message instead of taking the following lines with it

=item read the option form inside braces: C<< :key{:a<x>, :b} >> and C<:key{:!a}> next to the arrow form

=item a value that cannot be read no longer breaks the block: the attribute is dropped and the parser reports it on the root node as C<diagnostics>

=item read C<:key()> as an empty list and C<:key{}> as an empty hash instead of breaking the block

=item attribute value type is one of boolean, number, string, array or map; the untyped value tag is gone

=item attribute values in angle brackets hold a list when the content is separated by whitespace and a string otherwise; a comma inside angle brackets is part of the value, not a separator

=item attribute values accept the remaining delimiters from the configuration syntax table: bare single quotes, bare double quotes and corner brackets

=item add the C<< W<> >> contextual backlink markup code — same link shape as C<< L<> >>, rendered with a distinguishing class in HTML and JSX

=item html export emits C<:id> as an anchor on any block, not only on headings

=item markdown export keeps the checkbox state of task list items

=item a checkbox or numbering marker at the start of an item no longer drops the formatting codes written on the same line

=head1 0.0.52

=item keep the C<!> negation marker on list attributes like C<< :!allow<G> >>

=item parse an empty attribute list like C<< :allow<> >> without breaking the block

=item keep the C<|> url separator in a C<< L<> >> link when the display text opens with guillemet quotes

=item export C<parseFormattingCodes> — parse a string into inline formatting-code nodes without block-directive parsing

=item resolve C<< E<> >> entities in the HTML exporter, matching the JSX renderer — named, decimal, and hexadecimal forms

=item resolve C<< E<> >> entities inside an inline C<> code span, so literal angle brackets can be written as C<< E<lt> >> / C<< E<gt> >> without ending the code

=item render a pipe table nested inside a structured C<=begin cell> as a grid instead of collapsing it to inline text

=item add the C<=set> directive: assigns attributes to the next block, with delimited and multiline alias value syntaxes, continuation lines, transparency through directives and C<=comment>, per-scope targeting, and a warning when no target follows

=head1 0.0.51

=item remove the C<:fallback> resolver, C<materializeFallback>, and its provenance metadata

=head1 0.0.50

=item add fallback chain resolver with cycle and depth detection

=item add C<materializeFallback> producing a derived view where a resolved block carries C<:original-name> and C<:resolved-via> provenance and the source tree stays unchanged

=item render unknown Mixed-case blocks through their C<:fallback> target type in the Markdown and HTML exporters

=item resolve relative C<file:> image sources against a C<base> option in the Markdown and HTML exporters; absolute paths and C<https:>/C<data:> sources pass through

=item omit an empty C<alt> value in the HTML image tag instead of rendering C<alt="undefined">

=head1 0.0.48

=item add C<exports> map, top-level C<types>, and explicit C<sideEffects> to package manifest

=item C<toMarkdown> iterates into unknown Mixed-case named blocks instead of dropping them. The wrapper renders as a transparent container, its children pass through their own rules. C<exportHtml> behaviour unchanged

=item replace top-level C<require('../package.json')> with a build-injected C<version> module. The published ESM no longer carries a C<require> call, so browser bundlers (Vite/Rollup) can lazy-load the package without C<require is not defined> at runtime

=head1 0.0.47

=item C<=config item1>..C<=config item6> defaults now propagate to matching C<=item> blocks. The C<=config> propagation pass already handled C<=head1>..C<=head6>; the same C<item{level}> lookup is extended to items (plugin-items renames C<=item1> to a block with C<name=item, level=1>, mirroring the head case)

=item C<:numbered> inherited from C<=config> on items now promotes the wrapping list to ordered. When every item in a synthesized C<:list> has effective C<numbered: true> (either own or inherited), the list discriminator switches from C<itemized> to C<ordered>; an C<:!numbered> override on any item keeps it itemized

=head1 0.0.45

=item extend C<parseOpt> with optional C<mode: 'pod' | 'md'> field. Consumers (e.g. C<@podlite/core>) can dispatch to a Markdown parser when C<mode === 'md'>. Pure type extension; schema's own parse unchanged

=item C<=config> block defaults are merged into matching blocks' C<node.config> during C<toAst>. Own attributes still override inherited ones. Renderers and pre-render passes that read C<node.config> directly (e.g. folded-section grouping in C<@podlite/to-jsx>) now see attributes inherited from C<=config>. For C<=head1>..C<=head6> the lookup matches by C<head{level}> (@schueani in podlite/podlite-desktop#53)

=item C<:numbered> on a heading attaches an auto-numbered prefix to the head node as C<numberPrefix>. The pre-pass walks the document in source order, increments a per-level counter on each C<:numbered> head, and resets deeper levels on every head encountered. Renderers consume C<node.numberPrefix> and emit it before the heading text; C<exportHtml> wraps it in C<<span class="head-number">>, C<exportMarkdown> writes it inline. C<:!numbered> on a head overrides an inherited C<:numbered> default (@schueani in podlite/podlite-desktop#53)

=head1 0.0.44

=item allow single-letter identifiers (@schueani in podlite/podlite-desktop#53)

=head1 0.0.43

=item C<runSelector> predicates match attributes inherited from C<=config> defaults, resolved in source order

=head1 0.0.42

=item add C<=boundary> directive for marking structural section transitions. Optional C<:caption> attribute renders as C<title> on HTML C<<hr>>; Markdown outputs C<--->; JSX outputs C<hr> element
=item add C<=data-table> block for CSV/TSV rendering with C<:src>, C<:columns>, C<:rename>. Inline body and C<:src<data:KEY>> resolved at parse; C<file:>/C<http:>/C<https:> deferred to renderer
=item add predicate grammar to C<parseSelector>/C<runSelector>. Patterns are C<block-type[ :attr<value> ]> with wildcard C<*>, modifiers C<!>/C<?>/C<!?>, exact-match C<<v>>, list-contains C<~<v>>, AND via whitespace, OR via comma. C<ParsedSelector.blockFilters: string[]> becomes C<patterns: Pattern[]>
=item tighten C<parseSelector> scheme detection to identifier-shaped names so bare predicates like C<*[:attr<v>]> aren't consumed as a scheme
=item add C<G<>> guarded markup code. Production mode masks non-whitespace characters with U+2588; draft mode renders content visibly. Mode via C<toHtml({renderMode})> / C<toMarkdown({renderMode})>, default C<production>
=item add C<:masked> block attribute. Marks any block as guarded — content masked recursively in production mode, visible in draft. Idempotent with C<G<>>
=item add C<context> option to C<toAny>/C<toHtml>/C<toMarkdown> for passing initial render context to handlers
=item add C<maskText(s)> and C<collectText(content)> helpers
=item C<makeInterator>'s C<defaultFn> now returns the inner rule's result, enabling rule-chaining patterns (used by the masking interceptor)

=head1 0.0.41

=item expose C<attributesOnly> PEG start rule for parsing stand-alone attribute fragments (e.g. C< :line-numbers :caption<Example>>). Reuses existing C<attributes> rule
=item add C<parseAttributes(src)> helper — returns config items from an attribute string; empty or malformed input returns C<[]>. Used by C<@podlite/markdown> for fenced-code attrs

=head1 0.0.40

=item recognise the C<header> MIME parameter (RFC 4180 §3) on C<:mime-type> for CSV/TSV C<=data> blocks. When the C<=data> block declares C<:mime-type<'text/csv; header=present'>> (or the same with C<text/tab-separated-values>), C<=table data:X> marks the first row as C<:header>, which renders as C<<th>>/C<<thead>> in HTML and JSX. Default C<absent> preserves the previous behaviour. The MIME-type parser also accepts other parameters (e.g. C<charset>) without disturbing type matching. Spec: podlite-specs#25

=head1 0.0.39

=item resolve C<=table data:<key>> as CSV from a C<=data :mime-type<text/csv>> block (spec §1672) — table content is replaced with C<=row>/C<=cell> structure parsed per RFC 4180 (comma delimiter, C<"...">  quoted fields with C<""> escape). Spec §1672 does not define how to mark a header in CSV-sourced tables; authors who need a header row should use a structured table with C<=begin row :header> or a Markdown GFM table. C<file:<path>> scheme is left unresolved for host-driven readers
=item resolve C<=table data:<key>> as TSV when the C<=data> block carries C<:mime-type<text/tab-separated-values>> — split on tabs only, no quoting (C<"> is a literal character, unlike CSV)
=item add table error recovery — pad short rows with empty cells, truncate over-long rows; expected cell count is taken from a C<:header> row when present, otherwise from the row with the maximum cell count. Warn on each adjustment. Tables containing C<:colspan>/C<:rowspan> cells are skipped (naive count would mis-handle spanning)
=item warn on mixed column separator types within a single text-mode C<=table> (e.g. C<|> in one row, whitespace in another) — does not block parsing
=item improve CSV/TSV error recovery — missing C<=data> block leaves the table empty; a non-tabular mime-type is rendered as a C<=code> block so the underlying content remains visible
=item add per-line separator detection for text-mode C<=table> when lines disagree about separator type. A common pattern — header with C<|> for visual emphasis and data rows aligned by whitespace alone — used to collapse columns under the unified positional mask; each line is now parsed on its own and produces the expected cells. Tables with a single separator type, or with both visible kinds (C<|>+C<+>) handled by the shared mask, still go through the legacy positional path so continuation lines in multi-line rows keep their column alignment
=item move C<parseSelector> and C<runSelector> here from C<@podlite/publisher>. These are pure AST queries (no I/O, no filesystem) and belong in C<@podlite/schema>. C<runSelector> is now generic over a C<SelectorDoc> shape (C<{ file, node }>), so consumers without the publisher's richer C<publishRecord> type can use it directly. The publisher re-exports from C<@podlite/schema> for backward compatibility

=head1 0.0.38

=item added C<type: "map"> on config items parsed from C<:attr{k=>v, ...}> key-value syntax — previously the hash form returned the map value without a type annotation, making it indistinguishable from other value kinds
=item added C<makeAttrs().getMapValue(name)> helper — returns the first map-typed value as a plain object, or undefined when the attribute is absent or not a map
=item added guillemet bracket support («...») for formatting codes A, V, S, Z, D, X — previously only angle brackets (<...>) worked for these codes
=item refactored grammarfc to use parametric rules — unified simple codes via C<FCodeSimple> template, D/X via C<FCodeWithArray>
=item bumped Pegmill dependency from 0.1.0 to 0.1.2
=item added C<O<>> strikethrough formatting code (renders to HTML C<<del>>, Markdown C<~~...~~>)
=item replaced legacy C<Delete<>> alias with canonical C<O<>> — Markdown C<~~text~~> now produces AST C<{name:'O'}>. Helper renamed C<mkFomattingCodeDelete> → C<mkFomattingCodeO>
=item added C<H<>> superscript (renders to HTML C<<sup>>) and C<J<>> subscript (renders to HTML C<<sub>>) formatting codes
=item added structured table format with C<=begin row> and C<=cell> blocks, including C<:header> attribute support (renders to HTML C<<thead>/<th>>, JSX, GFM pipe tables)
=item implicit cell wrapping: non-cell children inside C<=row> are automatically wrapped in C<=cell>
=item unified table AST across text-mode, structured and Markdown inputs — all produce C<{name:'row'}>/C<{name:'cell'}>. Legacy C<table_row>/C<table_cell>/C<table_head> node names removed
=item added C<:colspan(N)> and C<:rowspan(N)> attributes for C<=cell> blocks. Values of 1 treated as default (no attribute emitted). Markdown export falls back to inline HTML C<<table>> when any cell has spans (GFM pipe-tables don't support colspan/rowspan)
=item tables with a C<:header> row now emit semantic C<<thead>>/C<<tbody>> wrapping in HTML and JSX. Tables without headers keep the flat C<<tr>> layout

=head1 0.0.35

=item added C<foldedLevels> field in C<Toc> type and C<mkToc> helper

